{
  "passage": "When we dig a well, we usually find water at some depth below the ground. This water is called groundwater. Many people imagine that groundwater flows in underground rivers or lies in large lakes beneath the surface, but this is mostly not true. Groundwater fills the tiny spaces between grains of sand and soil, and the cracks in rocks, much like water fills a sponge. When it rains, some of the water soaks into the ground. This process is called infiltration. The water moves down until it reaches a layer of rock that it cannot pass through. Above this layer, the spaces between the particles become completely filled with water. The upper level of this water-filled layer is called the water table. A layer of rock or soil that stores water and allows it to flow through is called an aquifer. The water table is not fixed. It rises after good rains and falls when more water is drawn out than is put back. In many parts of India, water is pumped out by tube wells for irrigation faster than rain can refill the ground, and the water table has dropped by many metres. Paving every surface with concrete also reduces infiltration. Rainwater harvesting, in which rainwater from rooftops is led into pits or old wells, helps to recharge groundwater.",
